%[text] # Taylor Series Expansion
%[text] This script demonstrates Taylor polynomial approximation for a function. We compare the original function with its Taylor polynomial expansion of various orders to see how the approximation improves as we include more terms.
%%
%[text] ## Taylor Series Formula
%[text] The Taylor series of a function $ f(x) $ centered at $ x\_0 $ is given by:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f(x) = f(x\_0) + \\sum\_{i = 1}^\\infty \\left. \\frac{\\text{d}^if}{\\text{d}x^i} \\right|{x\_0} (x - x\_0)^i&dollar&;$
%%
%[text] ## Step 1: Define the Function
%[text] We define a symbolic function and set the approximation parameters. We'll use the expansion point $ x\_0 = 0 $ (Maclaurin series) and compute approximations up to order $ N $.

syms x
f(x) = sin(x)*exp(x);

Interval = [0 3];
approximation_order = 9;
%%
%[text] ## Step 2: Compute the Taylor Polynomial
%[text] Using MATLAB's `taylor()` function, we compute the truncated Taylor polynomial of order $ N $. This polynomial agrees with the function and its first $ N-1 $ derivatives at $ x\_0 = 0 $.

fplot(f,Interval,DisplayName="Original Function",LineWidth=3) %[output:61085409]
legend(Location="bestoutside") %[output:61085409]
taylor_polynomial(x) = taylor(f, 'Order', approximation_order) %[output:43715089]
%%
%[text] ## Step 3: Visualize the Approximation
%[text] The solid line shows the original function, while the dashed line shows the Taylor polynomial approximation. Notice how they match well in the neighborhood of $ x\_0 = 0 $, but diverge further away from the expansion point.
hold on %[output:268f5945]
fplot(taylor_polynomial,Interval,DisplayName = "Taylor Order "+approximation_order,LineStyle="--") %[output:268f5945]
hold off %[output:268f5945]

